    "summary": "High‑energy New Speedway jam, fierce Lovelight finale",
    "blurb": "Features a scorching New Speedway Boogie → Nobody's Fault jam and a rowdy Turn On Your Lovelight finish. Jerry delivers ferocious guitar work throughout, while the rhythm section drives an intense second set. Standout moments include Candyman, China Cat Sunflower > I Know You Rider, and Saint Stephen.",
    "review": "On May 14, 1970 at Merramac Community College the Dead were in top form, launching a blistering first half that built into one of the most memorable second‑set surges of the early ’70 era. The acoustic opener gave way to a high‑octane electric set highlighted by Jerry’s “on fire” guitar on Schoolgirl and an incendiary New Speedway Boogie → Nobody's Fault jam that kept the crowd in a perpetual groove. The band rode that momentum into a tight sequence of China Cat Sunflower > I Know You Rider, followed by a soaring Saint Stephen and a rollicking Turn On Your Lovelight that even caught Pigpen’s impromptu shout after the drum outro. Throughout, Candyman shone early in the set while the rhythm section—Bill on drums with Mickey sitting out the acoustic numbers—kept the pulse relentless, and the second‑set extensions of Cumberland Blues, Good Morning Little School Girl, and a ferocious Lovelight cemented this show as a high‑energy showcase of the Dead’s early‑’70 fire.\n\nThe recordings vary in fidelity, but the Cotsman soundboard (gd70-05-14.sbd.cotsman.12439.sbeok.shnf) offers the clearest capture of the band’s power while preserving the raw edge that makes this night so compelling. Fans looking for the definitive listening experience should start with that source, then explore the alternate Warner‑Evans and Reelmaster captures for different perspectives on the same legendary performance.",
